1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,174
One map is a place.

2
00:00:03,074 --> 00:00:06,552
Three maps joined together are somewhere to go.

3
00:00:08,352 --> 00:00:11,830
Today the crew walks out of the yard,

4
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,020
through your pump house,

5
00:00:14,470 --> 00:00:19,687
and down a flight of stairs to a tank of standing water.

6
00:00:20,587 --> 00:00:24,500
And you stand the first things on your maps.

7
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:28,309
Here's an exit in the game.

8
00:00:29,209 --> 00:00:33,557
The crew walks onto the east edge of the yard,

9
00:00:34,007 --> 00:00:36,615
and they're standing in your gully.

10
00:00:38,415 --> 00:00:42,328
An exit is a mark with a special name.

11
00:00:43,228 --> 00:00:45,837
It starts with the word to,

12
00:00:46,287 --> 00:00:51,939
then an underscore, and then the key of the area it leads to.

13
00:00:53,739 --> 00:00:57,652
So in the yard I add a legend line,

14
00:00:58,102 --> 00:01:00,276
with the mark to gully,

15
00:01:00,726 --> 00:01:04,204
and paint one cell at the east edge.

16
00:01:05,104 --> 00:01:07,713
Then the same in the gully,

17
00:01:08,163 --> 00:01:10,772
with a mark that leads back.

18
00:01:12,572 --> 00:01:15,180
Three things to know about exits.

19
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:19,124
An exit takes whoever stops on it,

20
00:01:19,574 --> 00:01:21,748
so click the exit itself.

21
00:01:22,648 --> 00:01:24,822
Each person goes through alone.

22
00:01:25,722 --> 00:01:28,765
And they arrive beside the way back,

23
00:01:29,215 --> 00:01:31,389
never on top of it.

24
00:01:32,589 --> 00:01:37,372
This is the Beam app at the start of the game,

25
00:01:37,822 --> 00:01:42,604
before anybody has walked anywhere, and there's nowhere else on it.

26
00:01:44,404 --> 00:01:48,752
Last time, the transporter put you straight into the gully.

27
00:01:49,652 --> 00:01:51,826
That would spoil things now.

28
00:01:52,276 --> 00:01:57,493
A crew that can beam anywhere never has to find the way.

29
00:01:59,293 --> 00:02:02,337
Two lines in the header decide it.

30
00:02:03,237 --> 00:02:04,976
Known, and then no,

31
00:02:05,426 --> 00:02:08,904
means the ship doesn't know this area exists

32
00:02:09,354 --> 00:02:11,963
until somebody has walked into it.

33
00:02:12,863 --> 00:02:15,037
And beam, followed by no,

34
00:02:15,487 --> 00:02:19,835
means the transporter can never lock on there at all.

35
00:02:21,635 --> 00:02:24,243
The gully gets the first one.

36
00:02:24,693 --> 00:02:26,433
You type it yourself,

37
00:02:26,883 --> 00:02:30,361
because the editor has no box for it.

38
00:02:31,561 --> 00:02:34,604
This is the cistern on the handheld,

39
00:02:35,054 --> 00:02:38,533
a walkway round a tank of standing water.

40
00:02:40,333 --> 00:02:42,072
The cistern needs water,

41
00:02:42,522 --> 00:02:45,565
and the tileset has no such kind.

42
00:02:46,465 --> 00:02:49,943
So I add one line to the tileset.

43
00:02:50,393 --> 00:02:52,567
Water can be seen across,

44
00:02:53,017 --> 00:02:55,626
and it can't be walked on.

45
00:02:57,426 --> 00:03:00,904
That pair is what makes a map readable.

46
00:03:01,354 --> 00:03:03,963
You can see the far side,

47
00:03:04,413 --> 00:03:07,022
and getting there is the puzzle.

48
00:03:08,822 --> 00:03:10,996
Then a new area file,

49
00:03:11,446 --> 00:03:14,489
typed the way you typed the gully.

50
00:03:15,389 --> 00:03:17,563
A walkway round a tank,

51
00:03:18,013 --> 00:03:21,491
and a small room at the east end.

52
00:03:22,391 --> 00:03:24,565
It carries both header lines,

53
00:03:25,015 --> 00:03:28,493
so the only way in is on foot.

54
00:03:29,693 --> 00:03:32,302
The stairs are one more exit,

55
00:03:32,752 --> 00:03:36,230
inside the pump house against the east wall.

56
00:03:37,130 --> 00:03:43,217
In the game, the crew member who takes them comes out beside the water.

57
00:03:45,017 --> 00:03:49,800
Now, an area file can also say where its exits lead.

58
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:54,178
It's a block of its own, called exits,

59
00:03:54,628 --> 00:03:57,672
after the legend and before the dashes.

60
00:03:59,472 --> 00:04:01,211
Read this line as:

61
00:04:01,661 --> 00:04:05,574
the mark to cistern leads to the area cistern,

62
00:04:06,024 --> 00:04:09,502
and you arrive beside the mark called foot.

63
00:04:10,402 --> 00:04:15,185
You only need the block when the usual arrival isn't right.

64
00:04:15,635 --> 00:04:19,983
And the editor can't edit it, so you type it.

65
00:04:21,183 --> 00:04:22,922
Now the fact sheet.

66
00:04:23,822 --> 00:04:29,039
A record that stands on a map says where in two fields.

67
00:04:29,489 --> 00:04:31,663
The first is always Area.

68
00:04:32,563 --> 00:04:35,172
The second is one of two.

69
00:04:35,622 --> 00:04:37,361
At takes a cell:

70
00:04:37,811 --> 00:04:41,724
how far across, a comma, and how far down.

71
00:04:43,524 --> 00:04:45,698
So here's a trail sign,

72
00:04:46,148 --> 00:04:50,061
at twenty-eight across and five down, in the yard.

73
00:04:50,961 --> 00:04:54,439
In the game it stands by the exit,

74
00:04:54,889 --> 00:04:58,802
and clicking it puts its line on the handheld.

75
00:04:59,702 --> 00:05:01,441
Open the yard's map,

76
00:05:01,891 --> 00:05:06,674
and the editor draws the sign where the record put it.

77
00:05:07,574 --> 00:05:10,183
With Move I can drag it,

78
00:05:10,633 --> 00:05:15,850
and the editor rewrites that line in the fact sheet for me.

79
00:05:17,050 --> 00:05:20,093
The pump belongs to the pump house,

80
00:05:20,543 --> 00:05:24,022
so it gets a mark of its own.

81
00:05:24,922 --> 00:05:27,096
And its record says Mark,

82
00:05:27,546 --> 00:05:29,285
and the mark's name.

83
00:05:31,085 --> 00:05:34,128
Here's the rule that's easy to break.

84
00:05:34,578 --> 00:05:37,187
A mark's name goes in Mark,

85
00:05:37,637 --> 00:05:39,376
and never in At.

86
00:05:40,276 --> 00:05:42,015
At reads numbers only,

87
00:05:42,465 --> 00:05:45,509
so a word there reads as nothing,

88
00:05:45,959 --> 00:05:48,567
and the thing is never placed.

89
00:05:50,367 --> 00:05:54,715
Neither of these two has any words under its fence.

90
00:05:55,165 --> 00:05:56,904
That makes them scenery.

91
00:05:57,354 --> 00:06:00,398
They're drawn, and they're in the way.

92
00:06:01,298 --> 00:06:03,907
Click the pump in the game,

93
00:06:04,357 --> 00:06:08,270
and you walk up to it, and that's all.

94
00:06:10,070 --> 00:06:11,809
Last comes the sentry.

95
00:06:12,709 --> 00:06:15,752
Its patrol is a list of cells,

96
00:06:16,202 --> 00:06:18,811
with a semicolon between each pair.

97
00:06:19,711 --> 00:06:21,885
I've made its loop longer,

98
00:06:22,335 --> 00:06:26,248
and the editor draws it as a dashed line.

99
00:06:27,448 --> 00:06:29,187
Lint knows about exits.

100
00:06:29,637 --> 00:06:31,376
Misspell one of them,

101
00:06:31,826 --> 00:06:35,304
and it tells you there's no such area.

102
00:06:36,204 --> 00:06:38,813
Put a mark's name in At,

103
00:06:39,263 --> 00:06:41,872
and it tells you that too.

104
00:06:43,672 --> 00:06:48,020
It can't tell you an area is cut off, though.

105
00:06:48,470 --> 00:06:51,948
It only says a mark was never drawn.

106
00:06:53,748 --> 00:06:55,487
So play it through.

107
00:06:56,387 --> 00:06:58,561
Beam down, and open Beam.

108
00:06:59,011 --> 00:07:01,185
There's nowhere else to go.

109
00:07:02,085 --> 00:07:04,693
Walk east, and take the exit.

110
00:07:05,143 --> 00:07:08,187
Now the gully is on the list.

111
00:07:09,087 --> 00:07:10,826
Go down the stairs,

112
00:07:11,276 --> 00:07:13,885
and you're standing by the water.

113
00:07:15,685 --> 00:07:19,598
For the exercise, stand a crate in the cistern,

114
00:07:20,048 --> 00:07:22,657
and add a second way down.

115
00:07:24,457 --> 00:07:28,370
Next time, these maps get things you can use.
